Method 3: clone()

clone() is defined in java.lang.Object
If you call it, it will throw a CloneNotSupportedException();

How to unlock:
Lock 1. Make the class implement Cloneable

public Point implements Cloneable{ // Lock 1

	/**
* Allowed to change the return type to a compatible type, in this case a descendant,
* to avoid casting by the client
**/
	@Override
	public Point clone() { // Lock 2 is the override itself
		
		try {
			result = (Point) super.clone();
		} catch (CloneNotSupportedException) {
			//no handling code necessary
			//the existence of this try-catch prevents the exception from being thrown
			// Lock 3 is try-catch
		}
		return result;
	}
}
USAGE:
Point p = new Point(1,2);
Point q = p.clone();
- - at the above command, java calls a “native method” which means a method provided by the operating 
	system that bitwise copies a region in memory (Exact bit-to-bit duplication to a new memory location)
- - This method of copying is called a shallow copy because it only works for primitive variables
- - This will not work correctly with nested objects




