Day 9

Exceptions

1. Why do they exist?
Before the existence of exceptions:

Every potential had to be handled with an if statement in the method where it occurred. Otherwise,
the program terminates

With OOP, we could handle an error in any method on the stack

If main does not catch it, the program terminates

1.1 mechanism of exceptions:
	When an error occurs, we stop execution and create an Exception object which has
	As much relevant information as we desire about the error. This object is “thrown”
- meaning it is passed to ancestor methods on the stack, including the current method
- this is called the “Exception flow” of the program

- If the current method does not have a try-catch statement, the method is terminated,
And the ancestor to the next method is unsuspended from the stack
- If main() does not handle it, we have a program termination

We call them exceptions instead of errors BECAUSE they may just be “exceptional
States” that we may be able to recover from

The word “error” in the Java design philosophy is an unrecoverable event

There are hundreds or more named exceptions in Java, but only about eight errors
	E.g., ThreadDeathError

