3. General syntax for catching exceptions:

try-catch-catch-finally

One try clause
You can have as many lines of code as desired and may throw as many potential exceptions as desired
The first exception thrown immediately terminates the execution of the code in the try clause,
jumping instead to the catch clauses

As many catch clauses as you desire
An Exception object will linearly try each statement in turn until it finds one that matches it.
Then it is consumed by the first matching catch
Ancestor exception clauses consume descendant exceptio objects
