Two ways to store graphs:

Adjacency matrix and adjacency list

Adjacency matrix:
A[i][j] = true if (i,j) element of E(G), false otherwise
Storage: O(|V|^2) - num vertices squared)
Preferred when the graph is small or dense
	dense - meaning most of all possible edges exist
	small - can load matrix into cache, making lookups really fast

Adjacency matrix representation:
	find if common edge in O(1)
	find all neighbors in O(V)


Adjacency list:
array of vertices with a list of chained neighbors hanging from each vertex

Finding it two nodes u and v are neighbors of V: 
	check each linked list in parallel: one node from each list is considered before we increment
	O(min(degree(u),degree(v))

We can upgrade adjacency list to a hashmap of hash sets:
	O(1) to retrieve all neighbors of a node:
	O(1) to check if the other node is part of the hash set that is all neighbors

Finding all neighbors of V: O(degree(V))










